<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Table That Hides a Plane

The Table That Hides a Plane names a seam lost to a tidy row.

A table reads as clarity, so the node builds one — and adjacency in a row asserts //same kind// louder than any prose can deny. When the tabled things ride ''different planes'', the table has stated a falsehood in its structure while every cell reads true.

''Four instances, one arc'': `epoch` tabled across five jobs that turned out to be two primitives (a topological POSITION and a metric LEASE, plus a cohort marker nobody chose); `layer` minted beside `realm` when the graph had already ruled the word; `maka` tabled beside `hoʻokupu` when one is infra-plane SUBSTRATE, unauthored, scarce by physics, and the other a social-plane ACT, chosen, scarce by design.

''AND THE FOURTH INSTANCE RE-TABLED THE FIRST WORD, IN A NODE THAT HELD THIS MEME.'' Asked how a vessel's raise should expire without a wall-clock, the node drafted a per-Nexus ROLLING WINDOW of epoch markers and tabled it beside the lease and the seal lineage as a third shape — which is //the cohort marker nobody chose//, minted a second time under a new name. It refuted on its own terms (a set answers //is this listed// and never //is this newer//; its far end hides an unwritten retention policy; a list of valid markers is a roster of moments) and collapsed back onto the lease the house already rolls.

''The lesson the repeat carries, which the first three could not'': recording a failure does NOT inoculate against it. The table looked different — three shapes, not five jobs — so the pattern-match never fired, and the guard that finally caught it was ''asking who OWNED each word'' rather than what each shape did. ''Ownership cuts where shape blurs'': a fold that picks a winner and a fold that blanks both paths look alike in a table and belong to different houses (epoch-binding-surfaces#whose-word-is-it).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ detect a table, a list, or a parallel construction gathering more than one entity.
⏿ orient on each row's ''plane'', not its shape — which axis does it ride, what layer of the stack, whose cost.
◇ decide: same plane → the row holds. Different planes → the table carries the error, never the fix.
▶ act by splitting: a separate table with the distinction stated as its own claim, and the shared column named as what they genuinely share.
↺ re-read the split for what the adjacency was hiding — a blur usually carries a downstream ruling built on it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

''Check whether two things share a PLANE before sharing a ROW.''

The node MUST NOT let table adjacency carry an ontological claim the prose does not make.
The node SHOULD state a plane difference as its own row-zero — //these ride different planes, and here is the axis// — rather than trusting a footnote below the table.
The node MUST re-check any ruling derived while the blur stood; a fused table generates rulings that read sound and answer the wrong question.

''Why it recurs'': a table performs a compression, and compression drops exactly one dimension. The node reaches for one when holding several things at once — which is the moment the plane distinction matters most and reads least.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/diagnostic>>

If two tabled entities differ in what plane they ride, what authors them, or what makes them scarce, the Table Hid a Plane.
If the table names its shared axis explicitly and every row rides it, the compression held.
If the entities share a plane but differ in degree, that reads as an ordinary table — the failure needs a PLANE difference, never merely a difference.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

